 "note": "[agent-bulk] LEADING shower 18004 (103.5 MeV, 5 seg, pdg 11, len 16.8): clustering is correct. On shw18004-az-055-el+20 and az+085-el+20 the yellow member set is a two-prong blob sharing ONE apex at the X, plus two tiny on-axis fragments 50024 (0.33 cm, 18.0 cm / 10.5 deg) and 43017 (0.28 cm, 46.1 cm / 5.5 deg). Acceptance plot: five member squares and exactly ONE non-member circle inside 60 cm, 49023 at 15.7 cm / 34.7 deg, well above the 25 deg dashed step and only 0.46 cm long. No grey charge contiguous with the member set inside its cone, so nothing is missing; single apex, so not over-clustered. Vertex judged from vtx-az-055-el+20, vtx-az+065-el+20, vtx-az-055-el+60 at the event's own r95 framing (event_half_span_cm 126.1, no --vtx-span re-capture needed): the blue star sits at the lower end of the 109.5 cm muon 18001 (pdg 13, cluster 18, correctly left UNOWNED/grey) where the two EM prongs also start, so the vertex is fine. CAVEAT ON THE PAIRING, not a clustering claim: 18004's start is EXACTLY the main vertex (-85.1,-118.7,240.2) and its seed is in cluster 18, the same cluster as the muon, so it cannot be a converted photon; the reconstruction's pi0 group 0 = 18004 + 58029 (stored 122.9 MeV) rests on that object, and the vertex convention returns no chord for it (pio.kine_div: 'theta - -> m = -'). Same pathology as the pilot's evt280159 section 2.2. I did NOT re-pair it, because I could not name a better gamma1. SECOND FINDING - shower 24035 (40.7 MeV, 3 seg, pio_id -1) IS UNDER-CLUSTERED and this is the strong grey case: on shw24035-az-055-el+20 its yellow member set ends and a GREY, contiguous, collinear chain continues straight along the orange axis for at least as far again. That grey piece is 24009 (cluster 24 - the SAME cluster as the members - 16.6 cm long, tier 1, 7.28 cm / 10.0 deg, owner NONE, site from_vertices walk_exclude). On shw24035-acc it is the circle at (7.3 cm, 10.0 deg), inside the 12.5 deg dashed step. Marked 24009 IN on 24035. Weaker, NOT marked, and the ones I would let the owner reverse: 24012 (1.7 cm) and 24010 (2.95 cm, pdg 211) at 22.5 cm / 28.7-30.0 deg, just outside the 25 deg step but still grey and on the same ray. OPEN, deliberately not marked: 58029 (53.0 MeV) sits at 41.7 cm / 26.6 deg from 24035 on that same ray, so 24035 + 24009 + 58029 could be one photon shower split in three - but 58029 is owned by a neighbouring shower and I have no reconciliation test for it (correction 11), and merging it would dissolve the reco pi0, so I leave it as a question for the owner. NOT LOOKED AT: 12 of the 15 showers were out of scope (all below the 20 MeV kine_best cut) - 49023, 27015, 48022, 44018, 52026, 26014, 51025, 42016, 45019, 25013, 47021, 46020. This verdict is a claim about shower 18004, with a named finding on 24035, not an event-level all-clear.",
